  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Philippines Typhoon Haiyan 2013</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 8 November 2013 Typhoon Haiyan (local name Yolanda ) one of the strongest storms ever recorded made landfall in the eastern Visayas region of the Philippines notably the province of Eastern Samar. In the islands of Leyte and Samar most vital infrastructure were heavily damaged leading to basic services such as drinking water electricity transportation and communication being cut off. Initial relief assistance thus had to be rerouted to neighbouring provinces with functioning airports or seaports. Security concerns triggered by reports of looting presented additional challenges to the safe delivery of aid. WHO has categorized Haiyan as a grade 3 emergency (the highest level) with immediate risk of death from untreated injuries and water borne and vector borne diseases compounded by overcrowding of the displaced population. Majority of the basic health care facilities at the community/barangay level had been damaged. The national authorities placed several of the affected provinces under a state of calamity to help free up government resources for search relief and rescue operations and to implement price controls for essential goods. The Philippine government has also accepted international assistance for relief and recovery efforts.</narrative>
  </description>
